    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d362">

                <pb facs="0538" n="444" xml:id="pg444"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">612.112/66: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Chargé in Mexico</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Summerlin</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-03-15T17:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">March 15,
                                1924—5 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>147. Department’s 139, March 13, 6 p.m.<anchor n="21" xml:id="d362-f1"/> Mexican
                    Chargé d’Affaires called at Department this afternoon and stated that
                    instructions had been sent through Treasury and Interior Departments to all
                    customs authorities throughout Mexico to the effect that second payment of taxes
                    should not be required in cases where the first payment had been made in good
                    faith under compulsion to <hi rendition="#italic">de facto</hi> authorities. He
                    added that in cases where such payment had not been made in good faith each case
                    would be considered on its merits after careful investigation.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
